<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pohnpei</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ponape</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pohnpei is a volcanic island in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a> of the western Pacific, the largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a> and home to its capital, Palikir. Under the Saudeleur dynasty, from roughly the 12th to the 17th century, it was ruled from Nan Madol, a complex of artificial islets built of basalt off its eastern shore. It was later divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> under Nahnmwarki and came under Spanish, German, Japanese and American rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pohnpei.jpg" alt="Pohnpei" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aotearoa · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pohnpei_Island.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pohnpei, formerly Ponape or Ascension, is an island of the Senyavin Islands, part of the larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a> group. Its name comes from the Pohnpeian language and means upon a stone altar. It belongs to Pohnpei State, one of the four states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>. It covers 334 square kilometres, rises to 782 metres and is the largest island of the federation. Palikir, the national capital, and Kolonia, the state capital, are on it. It is one of the wettest places on Earth, and it is home to the ka tree, found only on Pohnpei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosrae">Kosrae</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlers were probably people of the Lapita culture from the south-eastern Solomon Islands or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>. Pohnpeian tradition divides the pre-colonial past into the Period of Building, before about 1100, the Period of the Lord of Deleur, about 1100 to about 1628, and the Period of the Nahnmwarki, about 1628 to about 1885. Legend says the Saudeleur rulers, the first to bring government to the island, were of foreign origin and that their centralised absolute rule grew more oppressive over generations. The dynasty ended with the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isokelekel">Isokelekel</a>, another semi-mythical foreigner, who replaced it with the more decentralised nahnmwarki system. Society was divided into five tribes, each headed by two principal chiefs.</p><p>The Spanish navigator Álvaro de Saavedra Cerón was the first European visitor, on 14 September 1529. The Russian explorer Fyodor Litke gave the first lengthy description in 1828. Whaling and trading ships followed, and a Protestant mission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> settled permanently in 1852. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic after 1854 caused a drastic fall in population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Carolines</a> by 1886 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de la Ascensión in what is now Kolonia. Germany bought the islands in 1899 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under German rule, from 1907, individual land deeds gradually replaced the feudal system in which all land was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. One work-for-taxes project on Sokehs Island sparked the Sokehs rebellion, in which nine people were murdered; 15 insurgents were executed and others banished to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a>. Japan took control after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> bypassed Pohnpei in 1943–1945, although warships shelled it. It was administered by the United States under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> oversight from 1947, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a> became independent in 1986. Pohnpeian is the indigenous language, and the island is the most ethnically diverse in the federation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pohnpei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Uranium-thorium dating of coral from the tomb of <b>Pohnpei</b>’s first island-wide ruler places its building at about 1180–1200, before monument building began on most other Pacific islands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudeleur-dynasty">Saudeleur dynasty · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
